{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" for number questions: the design, ready for when release work opens\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status: design only. Nothing is built.** Alex decided the behaviour twice:\n     4\t- D726, \"同意\": until the number answers pass, a first release shows number questions with their sources and \"Forecast unavailable\",\n     5\t  with the band, the forecast text and its history withheld together; \"it is built with the release work, which is not started\";\n     6\t- D756, \"结束这条线，预测暂缺上线\".\n     7\t\n     8\tOpening release work is Alex's call (council 72). This page makes the build a known, reviewed slice for that moment.\n     9\t\n    10\t**Revision 4 (council 72c) of revision 3: one structural rule instead of a list of leaks.** Rounds 72 and 72b each found new paths by which a withheld forecast\n    11\tcould reach a reader:\n    12\t- the idea card;\n    13\t- the closing text;\n    14\t- an idea summary that re-reads the ladder;\n    15\t- a release page that reloads from the live POC service;\n    16\t- a translated export;\n    17\t- a Yes/No explanation that quotes a number question's band.\n    18\t\n    19\tA rule that withholds fields reader by reader will keep missing one (AGENTS §1: \"when one mechanism draws findings round after round,\n    20\tstop answering findings: propose one structural rule\"). Revision 3 replaces that rule with an allow-list at one boundary.\n    21\t\n    22\t## 1. The gap, measured at $0 (2026-11-23)\n    23\t\n    24\tThe stored poc-v118 store `poc/data/comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-1/prod/views.sqlite`, read-only, gives:\n    25\t- **The number question v8 (FY2027 Data Center revenue)** exposes its band, label, condition, reasons, 75 judgements and 69 change rows.\n    26\t- **Its idea input** reads \"Bullish … $350 billion to under $400 billion\".\n    27\t- **Yes/No explanations quote number forecasts:**\n    28\t  - `old-3` `v36`, judgement 799: \"around $1 trillion\" for 2027 spending;\n    29\t  - `old-1` `v10`, judgement 865: the selected bands of both spending questions.\n    30\t\n    31\t  The judge is shown related questions' selected bands (`views/judging.py:208`), and its prose can repeat them.\n    32\t\n    33\t## 2. The structural rule: one release export, allow-listed\n    34\t\n    35\t- **One exporter writes the release data:** `release_export`, a single function beside `service.py`. It emits only the fields on an\n    36\t  explicit allow-list. A field not on the list cannot appear, so a new or overlooked path fails closed.\n    37\t- **The release builder calls `release_export` directly** and accepts no external input; a marker or version names a format, not\n    38\t  who wrote the text (council 72c). The finished package is checked as well as the page: it holds only the export's output, with no\n    39\t  market data, composite trials or translated file beside it.\n    40\t- **The release reads only that export.** Its page, its static build and its API, if any, read nothing else:\n    41\t  - no live POC service;\n    42\t  - no `/library` reload (`poc/ui/src/bootstrap.js` must not fetch in a release build);\n    43\t  - no prepared or translated JSON (`scripts/mock_translate.py`'s `data.en.json`);\n    44\t  - no `--composite-trials` input.\n    45\t\n    46\t  The static builder refuses any input not produced by `release_export`, which carries a marker and the allow-list version.\n    47\t- **For a number question, the allow-list is:**\n    48\t  - the question and its id;\n    49\t  - the evidence in force: one-line, quote, speaker, original day, tier and sources, amounts as quoted;\n    50\t  - the marker \"Forecast unavailable\".\n    51\t\n    52\t  Nothing else is listed:\n    53\t  - no ladder;\n    54\t  - no band, label or condition;\n    55\t  - no reasons or why_changed;\n    56\t  - no history, counts or assessment dates;\n    57\t  - no closing band (\"closed\" only);\n    58\t  - no theme pushes, no composite, no idea derived from its forecast.\n    59\t- **Ideas.** The release's idea composition (`idea.py`, `idea_composite.py`) runs on export data only. A number question enters it\n    60\t  as question and evidence with the marker. Its ladder is not read from storage in either the answer-format build or the summary\n    61\t  input (`idea_composite.py:255`, `:281`; `poc-v118-idea.json:94`). The test inspects the composed request itself, not only the\n    62\t  delivered card.\n    63\t- **The POC is unchanged.** Its API, MCP and page keep today's behaviour. Whether colleagues' POC page also changes is a separate\n    64\t  later choice for Alex.\n    65\t\n    66\t## 3. Identity: what a number question is (verified on four stores by council 72b)\n    67\t\n    68\tA view is a number question unless one of the following excludes it:\n    69\t1. **An event.** The view key's measure is in `identity.EVENT_MEASURES` (`event`, `policy_event`). That covers 85 of 85 event\n    70\t   questions in the three comparison stores.\n    71\t2. **A Yes/No question.** The ladder has exactly two labels which, canonicalised with the existing vocabulary\n    72\t   (`vocabulary.canonical`), are Yes and No in either order. The sealed ledger's 30 such questions use `是` and `否`. The Xbox `v37`\n    73\t   (`growth_rate`, bounds around zero) is Yes/No.\n    74\t\n    75\tEvery other view is a number question. Its forecast is withheld whether its bands come from `lo`/`hi`, parse from the conditions\n    76\t(the sealed `v3`), or cannot be resolved. An unresolved record is withheld and listed in the release check, with a key or without\n    77\t(council 72b: an `old-1/v8` copy without bounds does not parse, and its key still exists).\n    78\t\n    79\tCounts (council 72b), number / event or Yes/No:\n    80\t- `old-1` 19 / 28, `old-2` 17 / 26, `old-3` 18 / 31;\n    81\t- the sealed ledger: 43 parse as money, 21 are withheld as unresolved, 30 are Yes/No.\n    82\t\n    83\tNo wrong show-or-withhold result was found.\n    84\t\n    85\t**Events are binary too.** All 85 comparison-store events are also two-answer questions, including both known prose leaks, and a\n    86\tbinary event such as \"Restriction adopted\" / \"No restriction adopted\" exists in the served ledger (council 72c). §4's rule applies to\n    87\tevery question that is not a number question, events and Yes/No alike, and each keeps its answer label.\n    88\t\n    89\t## 4. Questions that are not number questions: an open choice for Alex (not decided here)\n    90\t\n    91\tD726 covers number questions. A Yes/No question's band is not a number forecast, but its stored explanation can quote one (§1). The\n    92\tallow-list must say which Yes/No fields the release shows:\n    93\t- **(a)** the question, its evidence and its answer (Yes/No and label), with no explanation text. Safe, and it removes the reasoning\n    94\t  readers would see.\n    95\t- **(b)** as (a), plus the explanation only for judgements whose request showed the judge no number question's state. Code can\n    96\t  decide this from the stored request (`views/judging.py`, which builds the related questions shown).\n    97\t- **Measured (council 72c),** matching each judgement to its archived request:\n    98\t  - (b) would keep explanations on 31 of 769, 44 of 776 and 43 of 958 judgements in the three comparison runs;\n    99\t  - for a reader at the latest answer, it would keep them on 21 of 85 questions;\n   100\t  - it would keep 73 of 73 in the sealed ledger.\n   101\t- **The limit of (b).** It cannot promise the prose quotes no number forecast. A request also carries the question's own previous\n   102\t  reasoning, and can carry another question's, either of which may repeat a forecast seen earlier. Both known leaks are in\n   103\t  `why_changed`.\n   104\t- **Recommendation: (a) for the first release** (council 72c; Claude agrees, withdrawing its lean for (b)). It is the only option\n   105\t  that guarantees no number forecast reaches a reader through prose. (b) remains a measured later step.\n   106\t\n   107\t## 5. Tests and the $0 release check (each names its row above)\n   108\t\n   109\t- **Fixtures (real store on a temporary path):**\n   110\t  - a number question with `lo`/`hi`;\n   111\t  - one with bounds only in its conditions;\n   112\t  - an unresolved one with a key;\n   113\t  - a Yes/No question with numeric bounds;\n   114\t  - an event question;\n   115\t  - a Yes/No judgement whose prose quotes a number band;\n   116\t  - a real closing switch;\n   117\t  - an idea.\n   118\t- **What the tests assert.** `release_export`'s output holds only allow-listed keys (a schema check that fails on any extra key). For\n   119\t  number questions it holds no band, ladder, reasons, history, date or closing band; Yes/No follows §4's choice. The static build\n   120\t  refuses foreign input, and a release page makes no network fetch.\n   121\t- **Mutants:**\n   122\t  - an extra field;\n   123\t  - a fetch left on;\n   124\t  - a ladder read in idea composition;\n   125\t  - the identity mis-sorting each fixture.\n   126\t\n   127\t  Each must fail.\n   128\t- **End-to-end acceptance (council 72c):**\n   129\t  - an idea whose only question is `old-1/v8`: the card keeps its sources and \"Forecast unavailable\", with no scored driver and no\n   130\t    substitute band (the current composite expects an endpoint for every scoring candidate, `idea_composite.py:214`, `:231`);\n   131\t  - in the same release package, the two known event answers keep their labels and carry no spending forecast under the chosen\n   132\t    prose rule.\n   133\t- **The $0 release check.**\n   134\t  - On `old-1/v8`, confirm the marker and the same sources in the page and in the export data.\n   135\t  - Exercise closure on a temporary copy with a real closing switch, after first confirming that the unrestricted response exposes the\n   136\t    final band there.\n   137\t  - Check ideas with a recorded or test transport, inspecting the composed request.\n   138\t  - Use `old-3/v36` and `old-1/v10` as the Yes/No prose cases.\n   139\t  - Use one unchanged event question as the control.\n   140\t\n   141\t## 6. Not in scope\n   142\t\n   143\t- The judging, opening and extraction pieces (frozen).\n   144\t- The closed v13s line.\n   145\t- Other open defects.\n   146\t- No prompt or pipeline change.\n   147\t- The POC's own behaviour.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/RELEASE-BUILD.md'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# \"Forecast unavailable\" release: build design for slice 1 (the exporter and the checked package)\n     2\t\n     3\t**Status: revision 4, for council round 74d. No code yet.** Release work was opened by the council under D439 (D758). The behaviour is\n     4\tfixed by D726, D756 and `FORECAST-UNAVAILABLE-DESIGN.md` (revision 4). This page holds the AGENTS §2 tables for the first slice.\n     5\t\n     6\t**Revision 2 answers `codex-r74.md`** (see `claude-r74.md`):\n     7\t- the pass condition is an origin check of every field against its stored source, not a string sweep;\n     8\t- a missing source item is refused;\n     9\t- the package is checked before it is moved into place;\n    10\t- the manifest is a closed set;\n    11\t- the bound is validated as a date;\n    12\t- narrow reads;\n    13\t- size and speed budgets;\n    14\t- tests that separate every branch;\n    15\t- `object`, `kind` and groups dropped;\n    16\t- no idea submission.\n    17\t\n    18\tThe evidence summary stays as extraction stored it (§8, disputed in `claude-r74.md`).\n    19\t\n    20\t**Revision 3 answers `codex-r74b.md`.**\n    21\t- The checker derives everything it compares from the store, never from the package: the date, each question's class, the records and their counts.\n    22\t- A missing source item refuses `check` too.\n    23\t- The files must be two regular, non-symlink files, with no duplicate JSON member names.\n    24\t- The staging folder has one owner.\n    25\t- The builder's refusal is tested through the builder.\n    26\t- The diagnostics read only the columns a store has.\n    27\t\n    28\t**Revision 4 answers `codex-r74c.md`.**\n    29\t- One date rule for build and `check`.\n    30\t- Questions are ordered by `created_seq, rowid`.\n    31\t- The build claims its output folder exclusively before staging, so the final move can only replace its own empty claim.\n    32\t- Cleanup sits where the staging path is known.\n    33\t- A report failure after publication has its own outcome.\n    34\t- The builder's refusal is proved through the real command on a scratch copy.\n    35\t- Tests 9, 13, 14 and 15 are sharpened.\n    36\t\n    37\t## 0. Scope\n    38\t\n    39\t- **Slice 1 (this page), $0:**\n    40\t  - the exporter `release_export` and its self-check;\n    41\t  - the package writer, the package check and the origin check;\n    42\t  - the release builder, which calls the exporter directly;\n    43\t  - tests and mutants;\n    44\t  - the $0 release check on stored stores.\n    45\t- **Slice 2, next.** The release page reads only the package: no live service, no `/library` fetch, no translated JSON, no composite\n    46\t  trials, no idea submission control or route. It gets a browser check of the `old-1/v8` screen, which completes D756's screen check.\n    47\t- **No idea submission in the release** (council 74). The idea path (`idea.py`) reads the ledger at every step and calls a paid model\n    48\t  per idea. The design's §5 idea acceptance (an idea card backed only by `v8`, and its composed request) is carried forward as pending\n    49\t  for the first idea-enabled slice.\n    50\t- **Not in scope:**\n    51\t  - the POC, its API, MCP and page (unchanged);\n    52\t  - colleagues' page;\n    53\t  - frozen pieces, extraction included;\n    54\t  - any model call;\n    55\t  - commit, publish, deploy.\n    56\t\n    57\t## 1. What is built\n    58\t\n    59\t`src/hivemind/release.py` is the one owner, beside `service.py`.\n    60\t\n    61\t- **Constants:**\n    62\t  - `FORMAT = 'hivemind-release/1'`;\n    63\t  - `ALLOW_LIST = 'release-allow-list/1'`;\n    64\t  - `FORECAST_UNAVAILABLE = 'Forecast unavailable'`;\n    65\t  - `PACKAGE_FILES = {'manifest.json', 'release-data.json'}`.\n    66\t- **`ALLOWED`:** the closed key set per record type.\n    67\t\n    68\t  | Record | Keys |\n    69\t  |---|---|\n    70\t  | envelope | `format`, `allow_list`, `as_of`, `questions` |\n    71\t  | number question | `id`, `question`, `open`, `class` (= `number`), `forecast` (= the marker), `evidence` |\n    72\t  | event or Yes/No question | `id`, `question`, `open`, `class` (`event` or `yes_no`), `answers` (the two stored rung labels, in position order), `answer` (the stored label of the latest judgement's rung on or before the bound, or null), `evidence` |\n    73\t  | evidence in force at the bound | `id`, `one_line`, `quote`, `tier`, `speaker`, `original_who`, `original_day`, `sources` |\n    74\t  | source recorded on or before the bound | `title`, `publisher`, `day`, `uri` |\n    75\t  | manifest | `format`, `allow_list`, `as_of`, `data_sha256` |\n    76\t\n    77\t- **`question_class(views_db, view_id, labels)`** (design §3):\n    78\t  - `event` when the view key's measure is in `identity.EVENT_MEASURES`. The key is read as `select measure from view_keys`, and\n    79\t    only when the table exists;\n    80\t  - `yes_no` when there are exactly two labels which, through `vocabulary.canonical('label', …)`, are `Yes` and `No` in either order;\n    81\t  - `number` otherwise: a view with no key, or one whose bands cannot be resolved.\n    82\t- **`release_export(views_db, items_db, as_of=None)`:**\n    83\t  - pins both connections (`bundle.read_snapshot`);\n    84\t  - resolves the bound with `service.resolve_bound`, refuses a store with no default day, and validates the bound with\n    85\t    `date.fromisoformat`;\n    86\t  - exports every view that exists at the bound (`service.view_exists`), closed ones included, in `created_seq, rowid` order;\n    87\t  - builds each record field by field from the table above, with column-limited SQL:\n    88\t    - `select view_id, question, created_seq from views order by created_seq, rowid`;\n    89\t    - `select position, label from rungs`;\n    90\t    - `select rung from judgements where view_id=? and publication_day<=? order by judgement_id desc limit 1`;\n    91\t    - `service.is_open_as_of`, which reads only `open` and `after_seq`;\n    92\t  - takes evidence in force from `service.evidence` and its fields from that record;\n    93\t  - looks up each source's `title`, `publisher` and `source_uri` in the items database by `input_id`. A visible source whose item is\n    94\t    missing raises `ReleaseRefused` (there is no fallback to the id);\n    95\t  - calls `check_export` on its own result before returning (AGENTS §3: the writer refuses).\n    96\t- **`check_export(data)`:** the closed-set check.\n    97\t  - Every dict at every level has exactly its record type's keys.\n    98\t  - Value checks:\n    99\t    - `format` and `allow_list` equal the constants, and `as_of` is an ISO date;\n   100\t    - `class` is in its set;\n   101\t    - a number record's `forecast` is the marker;\n   102\t    - `answers` is two strings, and `answer` is one of them or null;\n   103\t    - `open` is a bool and every text field is a string.\n   104\t  - It returns the counts it judged: questions per class, evidence, sources.\n   105\t- **`check_origins(views_db, items_db, data, expected_as_of)`:** the pass condition. It is independent of the exporter's code path.\n   106\t  It derives everything it compares from the store, never from the package.\n   107\t  - **The bound: one rule for both commands.** `expected_as_of` is given by the caller.\n   108\t    - The builder passes the bound it resolved: `service.resolve_bound` on the requested day, or the default when none is given; an\n   109\t      omitted day with no default is refused.\n   110\t    - `check` passes the package's `as_of` through the same `service.resolve_bound`, which refuses a day after a store's last\n   111\t      completed day. A legacy ledger with no completed days accepts any valid date in both commands alike.\n   112\t    - The package's `as_of` must equal `expected_as_of`.\n   113\t  - **The class** of each question is derived from the stored measure and labels (`question_class`). The permitted keys and values\n   114\t    are then chosen from that class, never from the package's `class` field.\n   115\t  - **Order and counts.** The questions are compared as an ordered list in `created_seq, rowid` order, as `bundle.library` orders\n   116\t    them (`old-2/v2` and `v3` share sequence 8). Each question's evidence is compared in link order (`evidence_links.id`), and its\n   117\t    sources in row order (`evidence_sources.id`). A duplicate question, evidence record or source therefore fails.\n   118\t\n   119\t  It compares every field with its permitted stored source at the bound, by direct SQL:\n   120\t  - the question set equals the views visible at the bound, with `question` equal to `views.question`;\n   121\t  - `open` equals the latest visible switch's `open`;\n   122\t  - for `event` and `yes_no`: `answers` equal the rung labels in position order, and `answer` equals the label of the latest judgement\n   123\t    rung on or before the bound, or null;\n   124\t  - for `number`: only the marker;\n   125\t  - the evidence id set equals the links visible at the bound, minus those whose latest replacement is visible, computed in its own\n   126\t    SQL. Each evidence field equals the `evidence` row's column;\n   127\t  - each source list starts from every visible `evidence_sources` row, in row order, with `day` taken from\n   128\t    `evidence_sources.publication_day`. Each row needs a matching `items` row for `title`, `publisher` and `source_uri`; a row with no\n   129\t    item raises `ReleaseRefused` (a left join, so a missing item is never silently dropped).\n   130\t\n   131\t  Any difference raises `ReleaseRefused`, naming the question, the field and the evidence id. It returns the number of fields\n   132\t  compared.\n   133\t- **`claim(out_dir)`:** `os.mkdir(out_dir)`, which is atomic and fails when the path exists. The build owns the empty folder from\n   134\t  then on. A folder that exists, empty or not, is refused before any export. `release(claimed)` removes it with `os.rmdir`, which\n   135\t  succeeds only on an empty folder, so a build can never delete content it did not write.\n   136\t- **`write_package(data, out_dir)`:**\n   137\t  - creates a staging folder with a name unique to this invocation, `tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=out_dir.parent,\n   138\t    prefix='.<name>.staging-')`. Creation is exclusive;\n   139\t  - writes `release-data.json` and `manifest.json` there;\n   140\t  - if a write fails, it removes the staging folder itself and re-raises, because the caller does not yet hold the path;\n   141\t  - returns the staged path. It does not move it.\n   142\t- **`publish_staged(staged, out_dir)`:** `os.rename(staged, out_dir)` onto the build's own claimed folder. On macOS and Linux a rename\n   143\t  replaces only an empty destination directory. If anyone put content into the claimed folder, the rename fails (\"Directory not\n   144\t  empty\") and the build refuses. The move therefore never replaces a folder this build did not create (verified on this Mac:\n   145\t  rename onto an empty folder succeeds; onto a non-empty one, errno 66).\n   146\t- **`check_package(path, views_db=None, items_db=None, expected_as_of=None)`:**\n   147\t  - the directory holds exactly `PACKAGE_FILES`, each a regular file and not a symbolic link (`os.lstat`), with no subdirectories;\n   148\t  - both files parse as JSON with no duplicate member name at any level (an `object_pairs_hook` that refuses a repeated key);\n   149\t  - the manifest has exactly its four keys, and its `format`, `allow_list` and `as_of` equal the data's;\n   150\t  - its sha256 equals the data file's;\n   151\t  - the data passes `check_export`, and, when the stores are given, `check_origins` with the expected bound;\n   152\t  - it returns the counts.\n   153\t- **`withheld_unresolved(views_db, data)`** is for the report only. It lists the number questions whose `service.ladder_bands(rungs)`\n   154\t  returns `(None, reason)`, with the reason and whether the view has a key. Its output never enters the package.\n   155\t\n   156\t`scripts/release_build.py`:\n   157\t- **`build --ledger --items [--as-of] --out`:**\n   158\t  1. resolve the bound once (`service.resolve_bound`, `date.fromisoformat`);\n   159\t  2. `claim(--out)`;\n   160\t  3. `release_export` at that bound;\n   161\t  4. `write_package` to its own staging folder;\n   162\t  5. `check_package` on the staged folder, with the stores and the resolved bound;\n   163\t  6. `publish_staged` onto the claim;\n   164\t  7. write `<out>.report.json` beside the package, through a temporary file and `os.replace`.\n   165\t\n   166\t  The failure outcomes:\n   167\t  - **Before publication** (steps 3–6): on `ReleaseRefused` or an `OSError`, remove the staging folder it created and release its\n   168\t    claim, print the reason and exit 1. No final folder exists, and another build's folders are never touched.\n   169\t  - **After publication** (step 7 fails): the package is final and checked. Exit 2 with \"package published and checked; report not\n   170\t    written; run `check` to regenerate it\".\n   171\t\n   172\t  It has no input path for prepared or translated JSON, and it takes no clock reading; the folder name is the caller's (D12, D13).\n   173\t- **`check <out> --ledger --items`:** re-runs `check_package` on an existing package, with the stores, the package's `as_of`\n   174\t  validated against the store (above), and the regular-file and duplicate-key checks. It leaves the package in place and rewrites\n   175\t  `<out>.report.json`. This is also the retry after a report write failed. Its report gives `check_seconds` and marks\n   176\t  `export_seconds` as not measured.\n   177\t- **The report:**\n   178\t  - the store path and the bound;\n   179\t  - counts per class;\n   180\t  - the unresolved list;\n   181\t  - the fields compared;\n   182\t  - bytes;\n   183\t  - `export_seconds` (build only) and `check_seconds`.\n   184\t\n   185\t## 2. State × event (one export, read-only)\n   186\t\n   187\t| State / event | What happens | What is written | Day used |\n   188\t|---|---|---|---|\n   189\t| Store's first day still in progress | `FirstDayNotCompleted`; the build stops | nothing | none |\n   190\t| Store with no completed day and no judgement, `--as-of` omitted | refused: \"no default day; supply --as-of\" | nothing | none |\n   191\t| `--as-of` not a valid date (`2026-08-99`) | refused by `date.fromisoformat` | nothing | none |\n   192\t| `--as-of` after the last completed day | `BoundNotCompleted`; the build stops | nothing | requested day |\n   193\t| `--as-of` valid, before the first visible question | empty `questions`; the report says 0 | package | bound |\n   194\t| `--as-of` omitted | last completed day (a legacy ledger: its last judgement day) | package | resolved bound |\n   195\t| View created after the bound | not exported | — | bound |\n   196\t| View closed on or before the bound | `open: false`, with no reason and no day | — | bound |\n   197\t| Closed, then reopened, before the bound | the last visible switch wins | — | bound |\n   198\t| Event or Yes/No not yet judged at the bound | `answer: null` | — | bound |\n   199\t| Evidence replaced on or before the bound | excluded; the replacement is in force | — | bound |\n   200\t| Evidence replaced after the bound | kept | — | bound |\n   201\t| Source recorded after the bound | excluded | — | bound |\n   202\t| Visible source with no row in the items database | `ReleaseRefused`, naming the evidence and input id | nothing final | bound |\n   203\t| Store written by a live run | not supported. The builder is given a snapshot (AGENTS §9), and both connections are pinned | — | — |\n   204\t| `--out` exists, empty or not | `claim` refuses before any export | nothing | — |\n   205\t| `check_export`, `check_package` or `check_origins` refuses | this build's staging folder is removed, exit non-zero | nothing final | — |\n   206\t| A write fails inside `write_package` | `write_package` removes its staging folder; the builder releases its claim; exit 1 | nothing final | — |\n   207\t| `OSError` while checking or moving | the staging folder is removed and the claim released; exit 1 | nothing final | — |\n   208\t| Someone writes into the claimed folder during the build | the rename fails (\"Directory not empty\"); staging removed; the claim is not removed because it is not empty; exit 1 | nothing of this build | — |\n   209\t| Crash while staging | the empty claim and a uniquely named staging folder are left. The next build to the same `--out` refuses on the claim until the user removes it | claim and staging only | — |\n   210\t| Crash after the rename, before the report | the package is final and checked; `check` rewrites the report | package | — |\n   211\t| The report write fails after publication | exit 2: the package is final and checked; `check` regenerates the report | package | — |\n   212\t| Two builds aimed at one `--out` | only one `claim` succeeds; the other refuses before any export | the winner's package | — |\n   213\t| Package `as_of` later than the store's last completed day (`check`) | refused by `resolve_bound` | nothing | package `as_of` |\n   214\t| A legacy ledger with no completed days, explicit day | the build and `check` both accept any valid date (one rule) | package | requested day |\n   215\t| A package file is a symbolic link, or a JSON member name repeats | `check_package` refuses | nothing | — |\n   216\t| The package's `class` disagrees with the store's (a Yes/No answer replaced by the marker) | `check_origins` refuses: class is derived from the store | nothing final | bound |\n   217\t| Store or configuration changed between builds | each package records its bound and data sha256; the report names the store path | — | — |\n   218\t| Legacy ledger: no `view_keys` table, `是`/`否` labels | no key, so no event; the labels canonicalise to Yes/No | — | — |\n   219\t\n   220\t## 3. Value × reader\n   221\t\n   222\tReaders of the package:\n   223\t- `check_package` and `check_origins`;\n   224\t- the release page (slice 2);\n   225\t- the tests.\n   226\t\n   227\tThe report's reader is the person building. No POC code imports `release`.\n   228\t\n   229\t| Value | Permitted source at the bound | Checked by |\n   230\t|---|---|---|\n   231\t| `id`, `question` | `views.view_id`, `views.question` | `check_origins` |\n   232\t| `open` | the latest visible `view_switches.open` | `check_origins` |\n   233\t| `class` | `view_keys.measure`, `rungs.label` | `check_origins` derives it from the store; tests and mutants check the identity rule |\n   234\t| `answers`, `answer` | `rungs.label` by position; the latest `judgements.rung` on or before the bound | `check_origins` |\n   235\t| `forecast` | the constant marker | `check_export` |\n   236\t| evidence fields | the `evidence` row; visible `evidence_links` minus visible replacements | `check_origins` |\n   237\t| source fields | every visible `evidence_sources` row (`publication_day` as `day`); its `items` row's `title`, `publisher`, `source_uri`, required | `check_origins` (left join; a missing item refuses) |\n   238\t| manifest | the data's `format`, `allow_list`, `as_of`; the data sha256 | `check_package` |\n   239\t| report (outside the package) | `ladder_bands` per number question; counts | read by the builder's user |\n   240\t\n   241\t**Never read by `release_export`:**\n   242\t- `rungs.condition`, `lo`, `hi`;\n   243\t- `judgements.reasons`, `why_changed`, `implied_outcome`, `reference`, `pushes_json`, `theme_pushes_json`, `cited_*`;\n   244\t- `view_switches.reason`;\n   245\t- `clean_ladders`;\n   246\t- `thesis_groups`;\n   247\t- `dependencies`, `successions`.\n   248\t\n   249\t## 4. New path × old checks\n   250\t\n   251\t| Check on the old path (`bundle.library`, `view_bundle`, `service`) | New path |\n   252\t|---|---|\n   253\t| `resolve_bound`: unfinished first day, future bound | same call |\n   254\t| `bundle.library`: no default day refused; `date.fromisoformat` | same two checks |\n   255\t| A view not visible at the bound is not served | same `view_exists` |\n   256\t| Sources visible at the bound; replacements applied | same `service.evidence`, verified independently by `check_origins` |\n   257\t| Pinned read transaction | same `bundle.read_snapshot` |\n   258\t| `item_lookup` falls back to the id for a missing item | refused instead |\n   259\t| A malformed `clean_ladders` row is refused | not read |\n   260\t| Corrupt judgement JSON is refused | no JSON column read |\n   261\t| The UI contract rejects unknown fields | the release's own `check_export`; the page's reading of it is slice 2 |\n   262\t\n   263\t## 5. Prior decisions\n   264\t\n   265\t- D726: number questions show sources and \"Forecast unavailable\", with band, forecast text and history withheld together.\n   266\t- D756: Alex's choice.\n   267\t- D757: the design, revision 4.\n   268\t- D758: the release is opened; §4 (a); order.\n   269\t- D12/D13: no clock.\n   270\t- D163: English product text.\n   271\t- D690: poc-v118 stays the product.\n   272\t- D261: the ruler is untouched.\n   273\t- D274: the extraction prompt is frozen.\n   274\t\n   275\t## 6. Tests (`tests/test_release.py`) and mutants\n   276\t\n   277\tThe fixtures use a real store on a temporary path (`store.create_view`, `store.insert`), with a real items database. Each test names\n   278\tits row above:\n   279\t1. **Closed set (§1).**\n   280\t   - Every dict in a fixture export has exactly its type's keys.\n   281\t   - Positive control: an export with one added key fails `check_export`.\n   282\t2. **Number with `lo`/`hi`.** The record carries the marker. No rung label, condition, `reasons`, `why_changed` or `implied_outcome` of\n   283\t   that view appears in any decoded package field.\n   284\t3. **Branch fixtures for identity and the report:**\n   285\t   - a condition-only number that resolves: `number`, and absent from `withheld_unresolved`;\n   286\t   - an unresolved number with a key: `number`, and present;\n   287\t   - an unresolved number without a key: `number`, and present.\n   288\t4. **Yes/No with numeric bounds** (`growth_rate` key, labels Yes/No): `yes_no`, with its answer label. A `why_changed` quoting\n   289\t   \"$1 trillion\" is absent.\n   290\t5. **Event with the served ledger's `v49` labels,** \"Restriction adopted\" / \"No restriction adopted\": `event`, with its label kept. A\n   291\t   `why_changed` quoting another question's band is absent.\n   292\t6. **Legacy store** with no `view_keys` table and `是`/`否` labels: `yes_no`.\n   293\t7. **Closing switch** whose reason names the band label: `open: false`, and the reason is absent.\n   294\t8. **Replacement and source visibility** at two bounds. A visible source with a missing item is refused.\n   295\t9. **Package:**\n   296\t   - staged, then checked, passes;\n   297\t   - a foreign file fails, and so does a subdirectory;\n   298\t   - a changed data byte fails;\n   299\t   - an extra manifest key with an unchanged data hash fails;\n   300\t   - a manifest `as_of` differing from the data's fails;\n   301\t   - an existing `--out`, empty or not, is refused, and a leftover staging folder from another build does not block a build.\n   302\t10. **Origin check, each from the store:**\n   303\t    - in a staged copy, the system's forecast text placed in an evidence `one_line` fails, naming the field;\n   304\t    - a Yes/No record turned into a number record, keeping the closed set, fails;\n   305\t    - a duplicated question, and a duplicated evidence record, each fails;\n   306\t    - a package whose `as_of` is a day later than the store's last completed day fails `check`;\n   307\t    - a legacy fixture with no completed days, built at an explicit day, passes a later `check`.\n   308\t11. **Builder end to end,** run as the user calls it (subprocess, `--as-of` omitted). The package is final and passes `check`.\n   309\t12. **Builder refusal, through the real command, on a scratch copy** (the AGENTS §4 mutation procedure; no fake in the suite):\n   310\t    - the unchanged command succeeds on the fixture store (test 11);\n   311\t    - on a scratch copy, the writer fault \"one evidence `one_line` taken from the latest judgement's `why_changed`\" is introduced.\n   312\t      The same command exits 1 with the origin-mismatch reason, no `--out` and no staging folder left;\n   313\t    - on a second scratch copy, the same writer fault plus the builder's staged check removed: the bad package reaches `--out`. That\n   314\t      shows the staged check is what prevents publication.\n   315\t13. **`check` with a missing item.** A finished package. Then a copy of the items database with one referenced item deleted, and a\n   316\t    copy of the package with that same source removed and its manifest hash refreshed. `check` refuses, naming the missing item. (An\n   317\t    inner-join mutant accepts this pair, so this case catches it.)\n   318\t14. **Two builds aimed at one `--out`, overlapping.** The test calls the module steps directly.\n   319\t    - Build A claims and stages, and has not published.\n   320\t    - Build B's `claim` refuses while A holds its claim, before B exports anything.\n   321\t    - A publishes. Its package passes `check`, and B removed nothing.\n   322\t    - A third case puts an empty folder at `--out` before any claim: refused.\n   323\t    - A fourth case writes a file into A's claimed folder before A publishes: A's rename fails, A exits 1, the foreign file stays.\n   324\t15. **Files:**\n   325\t    - a symbolic link in place of `release-data.json` fails;\n   326\t    - a data file whose repeated `one_line` member has the genuine value last, with the manifest hash refreshed so every other\n   327\t      check passes, fails on the repeated name.\n   328\t16. **Bound validation:** `2026-08-99` refused; a valid day before the first question gives an empty package.\n   329\t\n   330\t**Mutants,** on a scratch copy with `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1`; each must fail a test:\n   331\t- `condition` emitted on number records;\n   332\t- `one_line` taken from the latest judgement's `why_changed`;\n   333\t- the event test removed;\n   334\t- the Yes/No test removed;\n   335\t- `check_package` without the file-set check;\n   336\t- manifest keys unchecked;\n   337\t- `check_export` without the closed-key check;\n   338\t- `check_origins` skipping evidence fields;\n   339\t- `check_origins` taking the class from the package;\n   340\t- `check_origins` comparing sets instead of ordered lists;\n   341\t- `check_origins` with an inner join to `items`;\n   342\t- `check` without validating the package's `as_of` against the store;\n   343\t- the builder skipping the staged `check_package` (the scratch-copy pair in test 12);\n   344\t- `claim` replaced by an existence check;\n   345\t- the symbolic-link check removed;\n   346\t- the duplicate-key check removed;\n   347\t- the switch reason emitted;\n   348\t- the missing-item refusal replaced by the id fallback.\n   349\t\n   350\t## 7. The $0 release check (stored stores, read-only)\n   351\t\n   352\tStores: `old-1`, `old-2`, `old-3` (`comparison-2026-11-17/run/old-N/prod/views.sqlite`, items `items-comparison.sqlite`) and the\n   353\tserved ledger with its items database.\n   354\t\n   355\t**Budgets,** set before the first control (AGENTS §2):\n   356\t- the package's data bytes at most `bundle.library`'s JSON bytes on the same store and bound;\n   357\t- export plus all checks at most three times `bundle.library`'s seconds on the same store, and at most 120 s per store.\n   358\t\n   359\tThe steps:\n   360\t1. **Build each store's package at its default bound.** The build passes `check_package` with `check_origins`. Report the fields\n   361\t   compared, the counts per class and the unresolved list.\n   362\t2. **The decisive acceptance case** (council 74), on the `old-1` package:\n   363\t   - Morningstar's `$361 billion` estimate (`e2295`) is kept, its summary and quote equal to the stored ones;\n   364\t   - the genuine \"Bullish confirmation\" quote (`e1865`) is kept;\n   365\t   - the genuine analyst \"$390 billion\" quote (`e3597`) is kept, as the control;\n   366\t   - in a copy of the staged package, the system's `old-1` j1580 `implied_outcome` (\"About $390 billion…\") substituted into\n   367\t     `e2295`'s summary fails `check_origins`;\n   368\t   - the same text added to the manifest fails `check_package`.\n   369\t3. **`old-1/v8`:** `class` is `number`, with the marker and the same evidence ids as `service.evidence` at the bound.\n   370\t4. **The known event leaks at their own dates.** Export `old-3` at 2026-08-25 (`v36` j799 is the latest judgement) and `old-1` at\n   371\t   2026-08-28 (`v10` j865). Each keeps its original answer label, and j799's \"around $1 trillion\" text and j865's band text are absent.\n   372\t5. **Control:** one unchanged event question keeps its label and evidence.\n   373\t6. **Closing.** On a temporary copy of `old-1`, add a closing switch for `v8` in the store's own format (\"due …, frozen on … at\n   374\t   rung N <label>\"). First confirm that `bundle.view_bundle` exposes the reason with the band label. Then confirm the package shows\n   375\t   `open: false` and not the reason.\n   376\t7. **Diagnostics** (not the gate). For every question, take each string the package must not hold:\n   377\t   - rung labels and conditions;\n   378\t   - judgement `reasons` and `why_changed`, and `implied_outcome` and `reference` where the store has those columns (the served\n   379\t     ledger has neither; the report says so);\n   380\t   - switch reasons.\n   381\t\n   382\t   Search every decoded string field of the package for each, with no floor. List every hit with its question, field, evidence id\n   383\t   and the stored string it matched, and read each by hand. A genuine source quote, such as `e1865`'s \"Bullish\", is recorded as such.\n   384\t8. **Budgets:** bytes and seconds against the budgets above.\n   385\t\n   386\t## 8. A disclosed property, not built: evidence summaries keep extraction's wording\n   387\t\n   388\tThe release shows each evidence summary as extraction stored it; the release computes or converts nothing. A summary can make\n   389\tcontext explicit or restate a source figure by arithmetic, attributed to its source: `old-1/e3238` gives \"more than $40 billion in\n   390\tFY2028\" for a quote's \"more than double that next year\".\n   391\t- **Measured:** 25.6%, 25.2% and 25.4% of evidence in force in `old-1`, `old-2` and `old-3`, and 35.0% in the served ledger, carry a\n   392\t  number not in their quote. In a sample of 24, 23 are context: a year, a date, a quarter, a product name, an amount from the previous\n   393\t  sentence. One restates \"close to doubling\" as \"nearly 100%\".\n   394\t- **Why this is not the forecast D726 withholds.** Extraction never sees the system's forecast. Its request lists each open question\n   395\t  only as id, object, due day, question and a \"takes\" line (`placement.py:19-61`, `extraction.py:398-400`); no rung, label or\n   396\t  judged position.\n   397\t- **Council 74b** (`codex-r74b.md` §2) verified that extraction sends no rung, label or judged position, and that the census\n   398\t  reproduces.\n   399\t- **The disagreement that remains is narrow.**\n   400\t  - Codex reads revision 4's \"amounts as quoted\" as requiring source wording for a calculated amount attributed to a source, such\n   401\t    as `e3238`'s \"more than $40 billion\".\n   402\t  - Claude reads it as \"the release computes or converts nothing\".\n   403\t  - Both agree: no digit filter, no change to extraction.\n   404\t- **The course taken.** By AGENTS' rule for an unsettled disagreement, the cheaper, reversible course is to keep the stored summary.\n   405\t  The quote is shown beside it, so the source wording is on the reader's screen. A later version can drop the field from the\n   406\t  allow-list or change it.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\"'^#+ D(726|756|757|758)'\"\\\\b' docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"10991:## D726 · 2026-11-22 · Alex opens a judge version for related number questions ($0 gate first) and agrees to \"Forecast unavailable\" as the first-release interim\n11823:## D756 · 2026-11-23 · Alex closes the v13s line; the first release shows number questions as \"Forecast unavailable\"\n11842:## D757 · 2026-11-23 · Toward production: the \"Forecast unavailable\" release design is ready for when Alex opens release work (council 72–72d); two choices go to Alex\n11867:## D758 · 2026-11-23 · Under D439 the council opens local release work for \"Forecast unavailable\"; non-number questions show no explanation text (§4 a); the v13s checker defects are closed as won't-fix (council 73)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
